(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["caiib"] = {"qs": [["Advanced Bank Management", "Job rotation as a training method mainly helps employees to:", ["Gain exposure to different roles", "Avoid performance appraisal", "Get a guaranteed promotion", "Reduce their working hours"], 0, "Rotating staff across desks such as credit, forex and operations broadens skills and prepares them for wider responsibilities."], ["Advanced Business & Financial Management", "A project costs ₹1,00,000 and generates ₹50,000 at the end of each of the next 3 years. At a 10% discount rate, its NPV is closest to:", ["₹50,000", "₹1,24,343", "₹36,364", "₹24,343"], 3, "PV of inflows = 50,000 × 2.4869 = ₹1,24,343. NPV = 1,24,343 − 1,00,000 = ₹24,343. ₹50,000 ignores discounting."], ["Banking Regulations & Business Laws", "A contract of guarantee under the Indian Contract Act involves:", ["Two parties only: the indemnifier and the indemnity holder, with no third party", "Only the creditor and the debtor", "Four parties including a trustee", "Three parties: creditor, principal debtor and surety"], 3, "A guarantee has three parties: the creditor, the principal debtor and the surety. A contract of indemnity has two parties."], ["Bank Financial Management", "A loan exposure of ₹100 crore has a probability of default of 2% and loss given default of 45%. The expected loss is:", ["₹2 crore", "₹45 crore", "₹4.5 crore", "₹0.9 crore"], 3, "Expected loss = PD × LGD × EAD = 0.02 × 0.45 × 100 = ₹0.9 crore. It is covered by pricing and provisions, while unexpected loss is covered by capital."], ["Bank Financial Management", "Under UCP 600, banks examining documents under an LC must determine compliance on the basis of:", ["Physical inspection of the goods", "The buyer's verbal confirmation", "The documents alone, on their face", "The seller's credit rating"], 2, "UCP 600 says banks deal with documents, not goods. They check whether documents appear on their face to comply with the credit's terms."], ["Bank Financial Management", "A bank has rate-sensitive assets of ₹800 crore and rate-sensitive liabilities of ₹600 crore. If rates rise by 1%, net interest income will:", ["Rise by about ₹2 crore", "Fall by about ₹2 crore", "Rise by about ₹14 crore", "Remain unchanged"], 0, "Gap = RSA − RSL = +₹200 crore (positive gap). A 1% rise increases NII by about 200 × 1% = ₹2 crore."], ["Bank Financial Management", "A bank has total capital of ₹1,250 crore and risk-weighted assets of ₹10,000 crore. Its CRAR is:", ["8.0%", "10.0%", "12.5%", "11.5%"], 2, "CRAR = capital / RWA × 100 = 1,250 / 10,000 × 100 = 12.5%. This is compared with the regulatory minimum including buffers."], ["Bank Financial Management", "An exporter receives an LC and uses it as security to have his bank open a second LC in favour of his supplier. The second LC is a:", ["Revolving LC", "Back-to-back LC", "Red clause LC", "Standby LC"], 1, "A back-to-back LC is opened on the strength of an export LC, typically for an intermediary who needs to pay a supplier. A red clause LC allows pre-shipment advances."], ["Banking Regulations & Business Laws", "Under SARFAESI, a secured creditor facing resistance in taking possession of secured assets can seek assistance from the:", ["Civil court of the area", "Banking Ombudsman", "Chief Metropolitan Magistrate or District Magistrate", "Registrar of Companies"], 2, "Section 14 of SARFAESI allows a secured creditor to request the CMM or DM to take possession of secured assets and documents and hand them over."], ["Advanced Business & Financial Management", "The internal rate of return of a project is the discount rate at which:", ["Payback equals project life", "NPV equals zero", "Profit is maximum", "Cash inflows double"], 1, "IRR is the rate that makes the present value of inflows equal to the initial outlay, so NPV is zero. Projects with IRR above the cost of capital add value."], ["Advanced Business & Financial Management", "Which of the following increases a firm's operating leverage?", ["Higher interest payments on the firm's borrowed funds", "Lower fixed costs", "A higher dividend payout", "Higher fixed costs relative to variable costs"], 3, "Operating leverage arises from fixed operating costs. Interest affects financial leverage, not operating leverage."], ["Banking Regulations & Business Laws", "A complaint for dishonour of a cheque under Section 138 of the NI Act must be filed within:", ["Three months of the cheque date", "One year of dishonour", "Seven days of the notice", "One month of the date the cause of action arises"], 3, "The cause of action arises when the drawer fails to pay within 15 days of receiving notice. The complaint must then be filed within one month, though courts can condone delay."], ["Advanced Business & Financial Management", "A project costs ₹6,00,000 and returns ₹1,50,000 a year for 4 years and ₹2,00,000 in year 5. Its payback period is:", ["4 years", "3 years", "5 years", "4.5 years"], 0, "Cumulative inflows reach 1,50,000 × 4 = ₹6,00,000 by the end of year 4, recovering the cost exactly, so payback is 4 years."], ["Advanced Business & Financial Management", "The risk-free rate is 7%, the market return is 12% and a share's beta is 1.2. By CAPM, its cost of equity is:", ["14.4%", "12%", "13%", "19%"], 2, "Cost of equity = 7 + 1.2 × (12 − 7) = 7 + 6 = 13%. 14.4% wrongly multiplies the whole market return by beta."], ["Advanced Business & Financial Management", "Annual demand is 10,000 units, ordering cost is ₹200 per order and holding cost is ₹4 per unit per year. The economic order quantity is:", ["500 units", "1,000 units", "2,000 units", "707 units"], 1, "EOQ = √(2 × D × S / H) = √(2 × 10,000 × 200 / 4) = √10,00,000 = 1,000 units. At EOQ, ordering and holding costs are equal."], ["Advanced Bank Management", "McGregor's Theory Y assumes that employees:", ["Dislike work and must be closely controlled", "Are motivated only by money", "Avoid all forms of responsibility", "Can be self-directed and seek responsibility"], 3, "Theory Y holds that people find work natural, can exercise self-control and seek responsibility. Theory X assumes the opposite and favours close supervision."], ["Advanced Business & Financial Management", "A firm's EBIT is ₹50 crore and interest is ₹10 crore. Its degree of financial leverage is:", ["1.25", "5.00", "0.80", "4.00"], 0, "DFL = EBIT / (EBIT − interest) = 50 / 40 = 1.25. It shows that a 1% change in EBIT changes EPS by about 1.25%."], ["Banking Regulations & Business Laws", "Under the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, the District Commission hears complaints where the value of goods or services paid is up to:", ["₹1 crore", "₹50 lakh", "₹10 lakh", "₹10 crore"], 1, "Following the 2021 revision of pecuniary limits, District Commissions handle complaints up to ₹50 lakh; State Commissions handle ₹50 lakh to ₹2 crore, and the National Commission above that."], ["Advanced Bank Management", "A loan portfolio's monthly returns have a mean of 40 and a standard deviation of 6. The coefficient of variation is:", ["6.67%", "24%", "46%", "15%"], 3, "CV = standard deviation / mean × 100 = 6 / 40 × 100 = 15%. It expresses relative variability and lets us compare series with different means."], ["Banking Regulations & Business Laws", "A bank wants to start insolvency proceedings against a corporate borrower under the IBC. The application is filed before the:", ["National Company Law Tribunal", "Debts Recovery Tribunal", "High Court", "District Consumer Commission"], 0, "Corporate insolvency resolution under the IBC is initiated before the NCLT, which is the adjudicating authority for companies. The DRT handles individual and partnership insolvency."], ["Advanced Bank Management", "A borrower's 'drawing power' in a cash credit account is mainly determined by:", ["The borrower's net worth only", "Value of current assets less margin", "The number of years of banking relationship", "The interest rate on the loan"], 1, "Drawing power is computed from eligible stock and receivables minus the stipulated margin, and limits how much can be drawn within the sanctioned limit."], ["Bank Financial Management", "If GBP/USD is 1.27 and USD/INR is 85.00, the GBP/INR cross rate is:", ["66.93", "107.95", "86.27", "83.73"], 1, "GBP/INR = GBP/USD × USD/INR = 1.27 × 85.00 = 107.95. Dividing instead of multiplying gives 66.93."], ["Banking Regulations & Business Laws", "Under the Bankers' Books Evidence Act, entries in a bank's books can be proved in court by:", ["Producing the original ledger in court in every single case", "Certified copies of the entries", "Oral evidence of the branch manager only", "The customer's passbook alone"], 1, "The Act allows certified copies of entries in bankers' books to be accepted as prima facie evidence, so banks need not produce original books in court."], ["Advanced Bank Management", "Using Tandon second-method norms, what is the MPBF for a unit whose projected chargeable current assets total ₹500 lakh while creditors and other non-bank current liabilities total ₹200 lakh?", ["₹175 lakh", "₹225 lakh", "₹300 lakh", "₹125 lakh"], 0, "Method II: MPBF = 75% of current assets − other current liabilities = 375 − 200 = ₹175 lakh. Method I would give 75% of (500 − 200) = ₹225 lakh."], ["Bank Financial Management", "A bank's assets have a duration of 4 years and its liabilities 2 years, with liabilities at 90% of assets. If rates rise, the bank's equity value will most likely:", ["Rise", "Remain unchanged", "Double", "Fall"], 3, "Duration gap = 4 − 0.9 × 2 = 2.2 years (positive). With a positive gap, assets lose more value than liabilities when rates rise, so equity falls."], ["Banking Regulations & Business Laws", "The Cash Reserve Ratio that scheduled banks maintain with RBI is prescribed under:", ["Section 24 of the Banking Regulation Act", "Section 35A of the Banking Regulation Act", "Section 42 of the RBI Act, 1934", "Section 5 of the Banking Regulation Act"], 2, "CRR is maintained under Section 42 of the RBI Act. SLR, by contrast, is prescribed under Section 24 of the Banking Regulation Act."], ["Advanced Business & Financial Management", "In Ansoff's matrix, selling existing products in new markets is called:", ["Product development", "Diversification", "Market development", "Market penetration"], 2, "Market development takes current products to new customer segments or geographies. Product development offers new products to existing markets."], ["Advanced Bank Management", "The arithmetic mean of 4, 8, 15, 23 and 42 is:", ["15.0", "18.4", "23.0", "19.5"], 1, "Mean = (4 + 8 + 15 + 23 + 42) / 5 = 92 / 5 = 18.4. The median of the series is 15, a different measure."], ["Advanced Bank Management", "A card is drawn from a well-shuffled pack of 52. The probability that it is a king or a heart is:", ["17/52", "1/4", "4/13", "1/13"], 2, "P(king or heart) = 4/52 + 13/52 − 1/52 = 16/52 = 4/13. Adding 4 and 13 without removing the king of hearts double-counts it."], ["Advanced Bank Management", "In a series 3, 5, 5, 7, 9, 9, 9, 12, the mode is:", ["5", "7", "9", "7.4"], 2, "The mode is the value that occurs most often. 9 appears three times, more than any other value."], ["Bank Financial Management", "The Net Stable Funding Ratio under Basel III requires that:", ["Available stable funding is at least 100% of required stable funding", "High-quality liquid assets cover net cash outflows over a 30-day stress period", "Tier 1 capital is at least 4% of exposure", "Banks hold 4% of deposits as CRR"], 0, "NSFR ensures a stable funding profile over a one-year horizon. The 30-day stress test is the LCR, and the 4% exposure measure is the leverage ratio."], ["Banking Regulations & Business Laws", "RBI's power to remove a managerial person or director of a banking company in public interest is under:", ["Section 36AA of the Banking Regulation Act", "Section 42 of the Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934", "Section 13 of SARFAESI Act", "Section 138 of the NI Act"], 0, "Section 36AA empowers RBI to remove chairmen, directors or other managerial personnel of banking companies where necessary in public interest or to protect depositors."]]};
